<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
	<PropertyGroup>
		<TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
		<Description>The VpnHood app's API: the six interfaces a UI reads the app through, every shape they carry - state, features, settings, profiles - and the client that dials them over HTTP. A UI needs this and nothing else of the app.</Description>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<!-- Deliberately without VpnHood.AppLib.App: what is here is the contract, not the app. A UI
	     built on this package compiles without the engine, and runs against an app in its own
	     process or one on another device (VpnHoodApiHttpFactory) - which is what the WASM head does. The app
	     references this package to implement the contract, never the other way round.

	     The data shapes are shared on purpose rather than twinned per side: the app owns them and a
	     UI reads them, so a copy would buy a mapper per type and no isolation - rename a field and
	     both sides still have to agree. Engine types are twinned into here instead, by the
	     converters in VpnHood.AppLib.App, so that this project references no engine assembly. -->

	<ItemGroup>
		<ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\VpnHood.Core.Toolkit\VpnHood.Core.Toolkit.csproj" />
	</ItemGroup>
</Project>
